{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Macros - Overview}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Macros___Overview}
\pard\keepn \fs36\cf1 Macros - Overview
\par \pard \plain\f4\fs24 A macro is a sequence of Macro Instructions which are executed on user request to achieve a specific result.  Each macro is identified by a macro name.
\par 
\par Macros are collected together into a Macro Library from which they may be executed.
\par 
\par There is a macro Library per User Directory. Changing to a different User Directory changes to a different Macro Library. Note that any executing macros are aborted on change of macro library.
\par 
\par If a Macro Library does not exist when a User Directory is selected a Default Macro Library is loaded.
\par \pard 
\par Macro execution may be initiated from the User Interface, Special Function keys, Macro buttons on dialogs, Toolbar Icons and by the Macro Editor.
\par 
\par Any number of macros may run simultaneously, however only one copy of a macro may be executed at a time. Attempting to run a macro which is already executing will result in a \b Beep\plain\f2\fs20 
\par \f4\fs24 
\par One use of a macro is to restore machine conditions. This is achieved by a macro generator which is invoked either on shutdown when the user requests \b save conditions \plain\f4\fs24 or by requesting \uldb save state\plain\f4\fs24 {\v I6NCK5@sem.hlp} this inspects the current machine conditions and generates a macro to re-establish them.
\par \pard The conditions are therefore achieved by loading and executing the saved macro from file.
\par 
\par Macros may be created or edited using the \uldb Macro editor\plain\f4\fs24 {\v Macro_Editor} 
\par 
\par See also \uldb Macro - Contents\plain\f4\fs24 {\v Contents}
\par 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Macros dialog}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} 55E3Y}
\pard \f1\fs36 Macros dialog\fs24 
